# What Composer's dist zip carries, and what it leaves behind.
#
# `bin/split-php-client` mirrors this whole directory into the repository
# Packagist publishes from, and it cannot mirror only part of it -- `git
# subtree split` takes a prefix whole. So everything below is in the split
# repository, and should be: the spike is the record of how this package's
# arithmetic was settled, the generator is how its vectors are made, and the
# suites are how anyone checks the claim. That record belongs with the source.
#
# None of it belongs in an install. `export-ignore` is where those two answers
# separate: it governs `git archive`, which is what GitHub serves and what
# Packagist hands Composer as the dist zip -- the way essentially every
# `composer require` of this package will arrive. Cloning the split repository
# still gets everything.
#
# This file is inside the prefix, so it splits along with the rest and lands
# at the split repository's root, where the paths below resolve. The leading
# slash anchors each to the directory holding this file, which is `php-client`
# here and the package root there -- the same two directories either way.

# Development artifacts: a dated experiment and a Rust crate a Composer
# consumer cannot run at all.
/pda-spike      export-ignore
/vectors-gen    export-ignore

# The suites and their runner, the conventional exclusion. `conformance/`
# goes with them and is not a judgement call: it reads vectors-gen's
# vectors.json, which is gitignored and never shipped either way, so it could
# not run from a dist zip even before this.
/tests          export-ignore
/conformance    export-ignore
/phpunit.xml    export-ignore

# composer.lock deliberately stays. It is committed for a reproducible dev
# install (see .gitignore), Composer ignores a dependency's lock outright, and
# it is one small file -- excluding it would buy nothing and lose the record.

/.gitattributes export-ignore
